/-
# Minimal triple intersections and the Fitting subgroup (Kourovka Notebook Problem 20.122)

Kourovka Notebook, Problem 20.122 (V. I. Zenkov), marked solved in the 21st issue: for nilpotent
subgroups `A, B, C` of a finite group `G`, let `Min_G(A, B, C)` be the subgroup of `A` generated by
all minimal by inclusion intersections of the form `A ∩ Bˣ ∩ Cʸ`, `x, y ∈ G`, and let `min_G(A, B,
C)` be the subgroup of `Min_G(A, B, C)` generated by all intersections of this kind of minimal
order. (a) Is it true that `min_G(A, B, C) ≤ F(G)`? (b) Is it true that `Min_G(A, B, C) ≤ F(G)`? (c)
The same questions for soluble groups.
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Group.End
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Nilpotent
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Solvable
public import Mathlib.Order.CompletePartialOrder
public import Mathlib.SetTheory.Cardinal.Finite
public import WantedExt.GroupTheory.NilpotentThreeConjugatesFittingKourovka17Problem40Wanted

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.MinimalIntersectionsFittingKourovka20Problem122Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 20.122 (V. I. Zenkov). UnsolvedMath record
UM-00701780 (`KOU-20.122`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

The intersections `A ∩ Bˣ ∩ Cʸ` are taken over all `x, y ∈ G`, with the conjugates written `B.map
(MulAut.conj x)` (the same family as `x⁻¹ B x`); `Min_G` is the join of the members of this family
that are minimal by inclusion in it, `min_G` the join of those of least order (`Nat.card`); `F(G)`
is `fittingSubgroup` of the Kourovka 17.40 entry, reused here, the join of all nilpotent normal
subgroups, the largest nilpotent normal subgroup of a finite group. Both targets are stated for
finite soluble groups, part (c), whose refutation also refutes parts (a) and (b) for all finite
groups.
-/

open MathlibExt.GroupTheory.NilpotentThreeConjugatesFittingKourovka17Problem40Wanted
  (fittingSubgroup)

/-- The intersections `A ∩ Bˣ ∩ Cʸ` (`x, y ∈ G`) of `A` with a conjugate of `B` and a conjugate of
`C`, as subgroups of `G`. -/
def tripleIntersections {G : Type} [Group G] (A B C : Subgroup G) : Set (Subgroup G) :=
  {D | ∃ x y : G,
    D = A ⊓ B.map (MulAut.conj x).toMonoidHom ⊓ C.map (MulAut.conj y).toMonoidHom}

/-- `Min_G(A, B, C)`: the subgroup generated by the intersections `A ∩ Bˣ ∩ Cʸ` that are minimal
by inclusion among all such intersections. -/
def inclusionMinimalIntersectionJoin {G : Type} [Group G] (A B C : Subgroup G) : Subgroup G :=
  sSup {D | D ∈ tripleIntersections A B C ∧ ∀ E ∈ tripleIntersections A B C, E ≤ D → E = D}

/-- `min_G(A, B, C)`: the subgroup generated by the intersections `A ∩ Bˣ ∩ Cʸ` of minimal order
among all such intersections. -/
def minimalOrderIntersectionJoin {G : Type} [Group G] (A B C : Subgroup G) : Subgroup G :=
  sSup {D | D ∈ tripleIntersections A B C ∧
    ∀ E ∈ tripleIntersections A B C, Nat.card D ≤ Nat.card E}

/-- Kourovka 20.122(a), (c) (V. I. Zenkov): for nilpotent subgroups `A`, `B`, `C` of a finite
soluble group `G`, `min_G(A, B, C) ≤ F(G)`. -/
def minimalOrderIntersectionsInFitting : Prop :=
  ∀ (G : Type) [Group G] [Finite G], Group.IsSolvable G → ∀ A B C : Subgroup G,
    Group.IsNilpotent A → Group.IsNilpotent B → Group.IsNilpotent C →
      minimalOrderIntersectionJoin A B C ≤ fittingSubgroup G

/-- Kourovka 20.122(b), (c): for nilpotent subgroups `A`, `B`, `C` of a finite soluble group `G`,
`Min_G(A, B, C) ≤ F(G)`. -/
def inclusionMinimalIntersectionsInFitting : Prop :=
  ∀ (G : Type) [Group G] [Finite G], Group.IsSolvable G → ∀ A B C : Subgroup G,
    Group.IsNilpotent A → Group.IsNilpotent B → Group.IsNilpotent C →
      inclusionMinimalIntersectionJoin A B C ≤ fittingSubgroup G

/--
Resolved false: the Kourovka Notebook (21st issue) answers 20.122 "No, it is not always true in all
three cases", by an AI-generated solution of Kinyon and Urban confirmed by V. I. Zenkov: in their
Section 3.38 a soluble group `G` of order `36864` has nilpotent subgroups `A` of order `16` and `B =
C` of order `2048` with `min_G(A, B, B) = A`, while `F(G)` does not contain `A`. Stated for soluble
groups (part (c)); part (a) follows. Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved
Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem
20.122 (V. I. Zenkov); resolution: M. Kinyon and J. Urban, Results on problems from the Kourovka
Notebook, preprint, version 4, 19 September 2026, Kinyon-Urban, Section 3.38 (Problem
20.122(a)-(c)): G = E ⋊ Q soluble of order 512 · 72 = 36864, A = Z × H of order 16 and B = C the
stabilizer of order 2048 are nilpotent, min_G(A, B, B) = Min_G(A, B, B) = A and F(G) = E does not
contain A; the Kourovka Notebook (21st issue) marks 20.122 solved, "No, it is not always true in all
three cases" (AI solution confirmed by V. I. Zenkov),
https://kourovkanotebookorg.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/kourovka-mathematics.pdf.
-/
public theorem_wanted minimalOrderIntersectionsInFitting_refuted :
    ¬ minimalOrderIntersectionsInFitting

